# Phase 2 — Architecture Decision

**Objective:** Decide whether this capability is best delivered as a single agent, multiple specialized agents, or a coordinated multi-agent system, and define the high-level architecture, components, data flows, and trade-offs.

---

## Decision

> **Verdict:** Build a **coordinated multi-agent system using a supervisor (orchestrator) + specialist-agent pattern**, where deterministic validation logic (duplicate detection, vendor lookup, policy rules, FX) runs as **tools/services**, and LLM-driven reasoning is confined to extraction, anomaly interpretation, and explanation synthesis.

This is a **hybrid**: a small set of specialized agents orchestrated by a supervisor, with the bulk of authenticity/compliance verification performed by deterministic, auditable tools rather than by model judgment.

---

## Justification (mapped to the Requirement Brief)

| Brief requirement | Architectural implication | Why multi-agent (not single) |
|---|---|---|
| Heterogeneous inputs (PDF / image / structured) | Needs format-specific ingestion + OCR path | Ingestion/extraction is a distinct concern with its own failure modes and confidence scoring — isolate it. |
| Distinct validation domains (format, vendor registry, duplicates, policy) | Each is a separable check with different data sources & logic | Separation of concerns: each specialist can be built, tested, tuned, and audited independently; failures are localized. |
| **Explainability & auditability mandatory** | Each flag must trace to a specific check with inputs/outputs | Specialist agents/tools produce **discrete, attributable evidence**; a monolith blurs traceability. |
| **Reproducible / deterministic scoring** | Score must be computed by inspectable logic | Scoring is a **deterministic aggregation tool**, not LLM freeform — isolating it guarantees reproducibility (key open question #5/#6). |
| **0 auto-approval (hard)** | Terminal output is advisory only | A dedicated output/handoff boundary makes "never approve" a structural property, not a prompt hope. |
| Multi-currency normalization | FX reference + normalization step | Discrete tool, reused across policy and scoring. |
| ≤30s median latency | Parallelizable checks | Independent specialists (vendor lookup, duplicate scan, policy) run **concurrently**, which a single sequential agent cannot. |
| ≥95% extraction / ≥98% duplicate recall | Each metric maps to one component | Enables targeted evaluation & tuning per Phase 8/9. |

**Why not a single agent:** A single monolithic agent would (a) entangle OCR/extraction with verification, hurting testability; (b) push deterministic checks (duplicate, policy, FX) into LLM reasoning, breaking reproducibility and auditability; (c) prevent parallelism, risking the 30s SLA; (d) make the "never auto-approve" guarantee a prompt convention rather than a structural one.

**Why not a fully autonomous many-agent swarm:** Overkill for a bounded, well-defined pipeline. Adds coordination overhead, nondeterminism, and cost with no benefit. The decision space is finite and the workflow is largely linear with a parallel verification stage.

---

