# Phase 4 — Multi-Agent Orchestration Design

**Objective:** Define exactly how the Orchestrator and specialists coordinate at runtime — the orchestration pattern, message/handoff contracts, routing logic, shared state/memory model, conflict resolution, and termination conditions — and trace the end-to-end flow for the primary scenarios.

---

## 1. Orchestration Pattern

> **Pattern chosen:** **Supervisor (orchestrator) + staged pipeline with a parallel fan-out/fan-in verification stage.**
> Formally: a **hierarchical supervisor** whose control loop runs a **two-phase pipeline** — Stage A (sequential extraction gate) → Stage B (parallel verification) → Stage C (deterministic scoring) → Stage D (narrative) → Stage E (handoff).

### Why this pattern (not the alternatives)

| Pattern | Verdict | Reasoning |
|---|---|---|
| **Supervisor + staged pipeline (chosen)** | ✅ | Workflow is near-linear with one parallelizable stage. Supervisor centralizes state, audit, timeout/retry, and the **no-auto-approve invariant**. Deterministic, inspectable, easy to test per stage. |
| Pure sequential pipeline | ❌ | Would serialize the 4 verification checks → blows the ≤30s SLA. |
| Blackboard | ❌ | No need for opportunistic, emergent contribution; the evidence set is fixed and known. Adds nondeterminism. |
| Market / bidding | ❌ | No resource-contention or task-allocation problem to solve. Overkill. |
| Peer-to-peer agent chat | ❌ | Specialists never need to talk to each other; they only emit evidence to the supervisor. Direct agent-to-agent messaging would weaken auditability. |

### Key structural properties
- **Star topology**: specialists communicate **only with the Orchestrator**, never with each other. This is deliberate — it guarantees every handoff is logged and attributable (audit requirement).
- **The Orchestrator is the only stateful actor.** Specialists (A1–A6) and tools (T1–T2) are **stateless functions** of their inputs. This makes them independently testable and the whole pipeline reproducible.
- **The "no-decision" invariant is structural**, enforced at two points: the Orchestrator never writes an approval state, and the Handoff Boundary schema has no settable approval field.

